  <section><div class="container grid two">
    <a class="card card-link" href="/case-studies/lab-generic-plan-regression"><span class="kicker">Lab · Generated data</span><h2>Generic plan regression</h2><p>A plan setting hurt small tenants. Follow the change, plan evidence and tested response.</p></a>
    <a class="card card-link" href="/case-studies/lab-lock-queue"><span class="kicker">Lab · Generated data</span><h2>DDL lock queue</h2><p>An idle transaction, one ALTER TABLE and 14 seconds of zero reads, reproduced and measured.</p></a>
  </div><div class="container prose">
    <p class="small muted">These are synthetic labs using generated data in an isolated environment, not client incidents.</p>
    <h2>What a lab case shows</h2>
    <p>Each lab reproduces a kind of production problem I have worked on, using a fresh PostgreSQL instance and generated data. The <a href="https://github.com/kiransabne04/databasedeveloper.dev/tree/main/labs">setup and workload scripts</a> and captured output are public, so you can rerun them and check the numbers. The point is the reasoning: which evidence pointed to the cause, which response was tested, and how the result was validated. That is the same structure a client report follows; see the <a href="/sample-report">sample report</a>.</p>
    <h2>How to read a lab case</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Symptom:</strong> what an engineer would notice first, such as p95 latency rising for one tenant or reads stalling during a deploy.</li>
      <li><strong>Evidence:</strong> the plans, lock views and timings captured during the run, with the commands that produced them.</li>
      <li><strong>Cause:</strong> the explanation the evidence supports, and the alternatives it rules out.</li>
      <li><strong>Response:</strong> the change that was tested, its lock risk and how to roll it back.</li>
      <li><strong>Validation:</strong> the measurement repeated after the change, on the same workload.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Which service uses this reasoning</h2>
    <p>The generic plan regression is the kind of problem the <a href="/postgresql-performance-investigation">PostgreSQL performance tuning and slowdown audit</a> and <a href="/query-rescue">slow query optimization</a> look for. The DDL lock queue is what a <a href="/database-code-review">schema and migration code review</a> catches before deploy.</p>
